eny\"},{\"permission\":\"bash\",\"pattern\":\"git branch --delete*\",\"action\":\"deny\"},{\"permission\":\"bash\",\"pattern\":\"git branch --move*\",\"action\":\"deny\"},{\"permission\":\"bash\",\"pattern\":\"git branch --copy*\",\"action\":\"deny\"},{\"permission\":\"bash\",\"pattern\":\"git reset*\",\"action\":\"deny\"},{\"permission\":\"bash\",\"pattern\":\"git clean*\",\"action\":\"deny\"},{\"permission\":\"bash\",\"pattern\":\"git gc*\",\"action\":\"deny\"},{\"permission\":\"bash\",\"pattern\":\"git prune*\",\"action\":\"deny\"},{\"permission\":\"bash\",\"pattern\":\"git reflog*\",\"action\":\"deny\"},{\"permission\":\"bash\",\"pattern\":\"git push*\",\"action\":\"deny\"},{\"permission\":\"bash\",\"pattern\":\"git fetch*\",\"action\":\"deny\"},{\"permission\":\"bash\",\"pattern\":\"git pull*\",\"action\":\"deny\"},{\"permission\":\"bash\",\"pattern\":\"git checkout*\",\"action\":\"deny\"},{\"permission\":\"bash\",\"pattern\":\"git switch*\",\"action\":\"deny\"},{\"permission\":\"bash\",\"pattern\":\"git restore*\",\"action\":\"deny\"},{\"permission\":\"bash\",\"pattern\":\"git stash*\",\"action\":\"deny\"},{\"permission\":\"bash\",\"pattern\":\"git remote*\",\"action\":\"deny\"},{\"permission\":\"bash\",\"pattern\":\"git tag -d*\",\"action\":\"deny\"},{\"permission\":\"bash\",\"pattern\":\"git update-ref*\",\"action\":\"deny\"}]","time":{"start":1791639342351,"end":1791639342444}},"id":"prt_12606ccce001WSesN3OfuqEwGO","sessionID":"ses_ed9f94635ffee3zYlqCKAoMoF3","messageID":"msg_12606bdee001D4rU2JYTHuiuvP"}}
{"type":"tool_use","timestamp":1791639342904,"sessionID":"ses_ed9f94635ffee3zYlqCKAoMoF3","part":{"type":"tool","tool":"read","callID":"call_01_XBl1df93vm3YKZu6xi4C4257","state":{"status":"completed","input":{"filePath":"/workspace/docs/jobs/exist_notebook-journal/brief.md"},"output":"<path>/workspace/docs/jobs/exist_notebook-journal/brief.md</path>\n<type>file</type>\n<content>\n1: # Brief: notebook journal\n2: \n3: type: feature\n4: id: exist\n5: branch: feature/exist_notebook-journal\n6: date: 2026-10-10\n7: author: Leander Muskalla\n8: \n9: ## What\n10: \n11: Build the Journal surface in the notebook so the daily-journal mechanic the API\n12: now supports is usable in the app. The API provisions one hidden, system-owned\n13: folder per user (`title = '.journal'`, `is_system = true`, `is_hidden = true`,\n14: root-level); the notebook must present it as a special day-based interface\n15: rather than a normal folder.\n16: \n17: Notable changes:\n18: \n19: - **Mirror the new API contract.** Add `is_system` / `is_hidden` to the\n20:   `NoteCategory` type and the `PATCH /notes/categories/{id}/hidden` endpoint to\n21:   `apiRoutes.notes`, so the client can identify the journal folder by its system\n22:   flag and toggle hidden.\n23: - **Journal navigation entry.** Add a **Journal** destination to the notebook\n24:   navigation (desktop sidebar and mobile notebook drawer), beside Favorites. It\n25:   opens the journal interface and is never an expandable folder of notes.\n26: - **Journal interface.** Provide **Today**, **Yesterday** and **Other**\n27:   (datepicker into the archive). Opening a day resolves that day's entry by the\n28:   title convention (`YYYY-MM-DD` via `getUrlFormat`) inside the journal folder,\n29:   creating it on first open, and hands it to the existing note editor. No note\n30:   list — days, not names.\n31: - **Day reach.** From the notebook, only today and earlier are reachable; future\n32:   days are not selectable. The archive datepicker marks days that already have\n33:   an entry.\n34: - **Keep the journal out of ordinary notebook surfaces.** Journal entries must\n35:   not appear in the category tree, the notebook overview (newest / favorites /\n36:   last-updated), the no-category list, or the mobile notebook lists.\n37: - **i18n and tests.** All four locales in sync; extract any pure\n38:   day/entry-resolution logic into a testable service, following the repo's\n39:   existing `src/lib/services/` pattern.\n40: \n41: ## Why\n42: \n43: Users have no home for day-based writing: notes are name-addressed folders, not\n44: days. The journal gives one durable, always-present place to open today and\n45: write, reach yesterday for retroactive entries, and browse older days — the\n46: anchor of the connectivity direction.\n47: \n48: ## Out of scope\n49: \n50: - **Backend/API work.** The journal folder, its flags, `is_system` immutability\n51:   and the hidden toggle already shipped in the API job\n52:   (`home_notebook-journal-folders/`); this job consumes that contract.\n53: - Calendar day-marks / check-in linking (direction step 2) and the planner.\n54: - A journal-specific note endpoint or a date field on notes — entries stay\n55:   ordinary notes addressed by the title convention.\n56: - Adopting or renaming pre-existing `.journal` folders; recursive/descendant\n57:   hiding; search over journal entries.\n58: - Exposing the hidden toggle for ordinary folders in the UI (the endpoint is\n59:   wired, but only the journal uses it in this job).\n60: \n61: ## Notes\n62: \n63: - Journal identity is the folder's `is_system` flag, not its title, so a rename\n64:   (which the API forbids anyway) can't break the interface. Resolve the folder id\n65:   from `GET /notes/categories` and keep it out of the rendered tree.\n66: - The date convention is `getUrlFormat` (`YYYY-MM-DD`), the same as check-in;\n67:   entry titles equal the date. `NoteExportService` already exports the journal\n68:   folder — nothing to change there.\n69: - The dashboard's recent-notes widget reads `GET /notes/newest`, which the API\n70:   already filters; the notebook overview is computed client-side from\n71:   `GET /notes` and must be filtered here.\n72: - UI decisions the direction leaves open, to settle during implementation:\n73:   today opens write-ready, a past day opens the same editor read-first (no forced\n74:   focus); the datepicker marks days with entries and disables future days. These\n75:   are not blockers.\n76: - Route/naming is a developer choice; the check-in date route\n77:   (`/check-in/date/[date]`) is a close precedent if a date-addressed route fits.\n78: - Verify with `npm run check`, `npm run lint`, `npm test`; keep the four i18n\n79:   tables in sync (`npm run check` enforces it) and add unit tests for any\n80:   extracted day/entry-resolution logic.\n\n(End of file - total 80 lines)\n</content>\n\n<system-reminder>\nInstructions from: /workspace/docs/AGENTS.md\n# solyto frontend\n\n<!--\nThis is your project context, loaded by the agent at the start of every session.\nmanigot is vendor-agnostic: it runs Claude Code or OpenCode against the same\nproject (`mg --profile claude-pro` vs `mg --profile zai`/`--profile\nopencode-go`), and this one file serves both — manigot mounts it read-only\nwherever the selected tool looks for it\n(/workspace/AGENTS.md for OpenCode, /workspace/.claude/CLAUDE.md for Claude\nCode). Those mount paths are read-only: to change this context, edit this\nfile (docs/AGENTS.md), never the mount paths.\nThe same global agents are available under @name either way, and custom\nproject agents in docs/agents/ work under both tools — write them in the\nbuilt-in format (name:, description:, tools: Read, Grep, ...), no per-tool\nformat needed. To make a custom agent read-only under OpenCode, add a\n`permission:` frontmatter block (the built-in format manigot's conversion\npasses through to OpenCode's schema — see the manigot README's agent section);\nthe read-only built-in agents' blocks deny the destructive git commands\n(worktree management, branch -d/-D, reset, checkout, push, ...).\nCustom agents that must commit (like the built-in developer/reviewer/quality)\ndeclare `commit: true` in their frontmatter; agents that never commit declare\n`commit: false` and get a read-only git mount. The default — no agent named,\nfile missing, or marker absent/unknown — is a writable git mount, so a\ncommitting agent is never broken by a missing marker.\nAgent sessions also restrict git to reading history and making commits (the\nsession git shim): worktree management, branch deletes, resets, checkouts,\npushes, and the other destructive subcommands are refused.\nKeep this file tool-neutral — write it for \"the agent\", not for one vendor.\n-->\n\nThe client for [solyto.app](https://solyto.app): a free, private, all-in-one personal management app — todos, notes, calendar, contacts, media libraries, check-in, finances, feeds, time tracking, clipboard, and dev-requests in one place. This repo is the frontend only: a SvelteKit 2 / Svelte 5 single-page app that runs on the web, as an installable PWA, or as a native desktop/mobile shell. There is no backend or database here — every piece of data comes from the solyto REST API, which lives in a separate repository.\n\n## Stack\n- Backend: none in this repo — pure client-side SPA. Consumes the solyto REST API at `/api/v1` (`api.solyto.app` in prod, `http://localhost:8000` in dev; configurable per hostname in `src/lib/config/platform.ts`).\n- Frontend: SvelteKit 2, Svelte 5 (runes, `$state`), TypeScript (strict), Vite 7, Tailwind CSS v4 via `@tailwindcss/vite`, adapter-node (outputs to `build/`).\n- Database: none — persistence is owned by the backend. The client only stores auth tokens, theme, and preferences in LocalStorage (`src/lib/services/LocalStorageService.ts`).\n- Key packages: `@tiptap/*` (notes rich-text editor), `echarts` (statistics charts), `@simplewebauthn/browser` (passkey login), `marked` + `dompurify` (markdown), `@lucide/svelte` (icons), `svelte-dnd-action` / `svelte-gestures` (drag & drop, swipe), `canvas-confetti`, `@fontsource/*`, `@vvo/tzdb`.\n\n## Architecture\n- `src/routes/` — SvelteKit file-based routes, all client-rendered (no `+page.server.ts`/`+server.ts` anywhere). The root `+layout.svelte` bootstraps every state store via `setContext`, guards unauthenticated access (redirect to `/auth/login` or `/setup`), and renders the navbar/footer shells. Feature routes: `/todos`, `/notes`, `/calendar`, `/contacts`, `/check-in`, `/finances`, `/feeds`, `/time-tracking`, `/clipboard`, `/dev-requests`, `/libraries/{books,movies,music,games,links,quotes,recipes,plants}`, plus `/settings`, `/admin`, `/profile`, `/auth/*`, `/setup`, `/share`.\n- `src/lib/state/` — one Svelte 5 runes class per domain (`Auth`, `Todos`, `Notes`, `Calendars`, `BookLibrary`, ...). Instances are created in `+layout.svelte` and shared through Svelte context (`setContext`/`getContext`); each store fetches via `ApiService` and holds the result in reactive `$state`.\n- `src/lib/services/` — `ApiService` (fetch wrapper over the REST API: Bearer auth, JSON, `%s`/`%d` URL placeholders, FormData uploads) and pure domain services (`TodoFilterService`, `TodoSortingService`, `TodoGroupingService`, `TodoRelevanceService`, `LibraryFilterService`, `WealthStatisticsService`, ...) that are unit-tested.\n- `src/lib/config/` — `apiRoutes.ts` (all endpoints, composed from `getApiUrl()` + `/api/v1`), `platform.ts` (web vs. desktop vs. mobile via `PUBLIC_DESKTOP`/`PUBLIC_MOBILE`, API URL resolution), `themes.ts`, `navigation.ts`, `urls.ts`, `features.ts`.\n- `src/lib/components/` — feature components plus shared `ui/` (buttons, modals, navbar), `forms/` (inputs, pickers, toggles), and `charts/` (echarts wrappers). Several features keep separate desktop/mobile view variants (e.g. `calendars/views/{day,week,month,list}`, `todos/views/`).\n- `src/lib/types/` — TypeScript interfaces mirroring the API data model, one file per domain (`todo.ts`, `note.ts`, `library_*.ts`, `calendar.ts`, ...).\n- `src/lib/i18n/` — translation tables `en.ts`, `de.ts`, `es.ts`, `fr.ts`, typed via `types/translation.ts`; every UI string must exist in all four.\n- `src/lib/helpers/` — pure utilities (DateHelper, NumberHelper, FormatHelper, ColorHelper, MusicTheory, ...).\n- `src/service-worker.ts` — PWA service worker (app-shell precache, stale-while-revalidate, push notifications); `static/site.webmanifest` is the PWA manifest, `static/themes/*` are runtime-loaded theme CSS.\n- `tests/` — Vitest unit tests for pure helpers/services only (no component or integration tests); run in node env with `TZ=UTC` and `$lib` aliased to `src/lib`.\n- Data flow: component → state store → `ApiService` → REST API. There is no server-side rendering of app data.\n\n## Commands\n- `npm run dev` — start the Vite dev server on port 5173 (expects the API at `http://localhost:8000`)\n- `npm run build` — production build via `vite build` (adapter-node, output in `build/`)\n- `npm run preview` — preview the production build locally\n- `npm run check` — type-check with `svelte-check` (a11y warnings selectively ignored)\n- `npm run lint` — `prettier --check .` + `eslint .`\n- `npm run format` — auto-format with `prettier --write .`\n- `npm test` / `npm run test:watch` — run Vitest unit tests / watch mode\n- `make deploy` / `make audit` — Ansible deploy (external `deployment/` repo) / `npm audit` + `npm outdated`\n\n## Hard rules\n- NEVER modify files outside /workspace\n- NEVER install packages without asking (dependencies are pinned, `engine-strict=true`)\n- NEVER commit secrets: auth tokens, API keys, or `.env*` files (gitignored); never log or print token or user data\n- NEVER hand-edit generated output — `build/` and `.svelte-kit/` are regenerated by `npm run build` / `npm run check`\n- When adding or changing UI strings, keep all four i18n files (`en`, `de`, `es`, `fr`) in sync — `npm run check` enforces it\n- When scope is unclear: ask, don't guess\n- Do not refactor things unrelated to the current task\n- Do not add abstractions not already present in the codebase\n\n</system-reminder>","metadata":{"preview":"# Brief: notebook journal\n\ntype: feature\nid: exist\nbranch: feature/exist_notebook-journal\ndate: 2026-10-10\nauthor: Leander Muskalla\n\n## What\n\nBuild the Journal surface in the notebook so the daily-journal mechanic the API\nnow supports is usable in the app. The API provisions one hidden, system-owned\nfolder per user (`title = '.journal'`, `is_system = true`, `is_hidden = true`,\nroot-level); the notebook must present it as a special day-based interface\nrather than a normal folder.\n\nNotable changes:\n\n- **Mirror the new API contract.** Add `is_system` / `is_hidden` to the\n  `NoteCategory` type and the `PATCH /notes/categories/{id}/hidden` endpoint to","truncated":false,"loaded":["/workspace/docs/AGENTS.md"],"display":{"type":"file","path":"/workspace/docs/jobs/exist_notebook-journal/brief.md","text":"# Brief: notebook journal\n\ntype: feature\nid: exist\nbranch: feature/exist_notebook-journal\ndate: 2026-10-10\nauthor: Leander Muskalla\n\n## What\n\nBuild the Journal surface in the notebook so the daily-journal mechanic the API\nnow supports is usable in the app. The API provisions one hidden, system-owned\nfolder per user (`title = '.journal'`, `is_system = true`, `is_hidden = true`,\nroot-level); the notebook must present it as a special day-based interface\nrather than a normal folder.\n\nNotable changes:\n\n- **Mirror the new API contract.** Add `is_system` / `is_hidden` to the\n  `NoteCategory` type and the `PATCH /notes/categories/{id}/hidden` endpoint to\n  `apiRoutes.notes`, so the client can identify the journal folder by its system\n  flag and toggle hidden.\n- **Journal navigation entry.** Add a **Journal** destination to the notebook\n  navigation (desktop sidebar and mobile notebook drawer), beside Favorites. It\n  opens the journal interface and is never an expandable folder of notes.\n- **Journal interface.** Provide **Today**, **Yesterday** and **Other**\n  (datepicker into the archive). Opening a day resolves that day's entry by the\n  title convention (`YYYY-MM-DD` via `getUrlFormat`) inside the journal folder,\n  creating it on first open, and hands it to the existing note editor. No note\n  list — days, not names.\n- **Day reach.** From the notebook, only today and earlier are reachable; future\n  days are not selectable. The archive datepicker marks days that already have\n  an entry.\n- **Keep the journal out of ordinary notebook surfaces.** Journal entries must\n  not appear in the category tree, the notebook overview (newest / favorites /\n  last-updated), the no-category list, or the mobile notebook lists.\n- **i18n and tests.** All four locales in sync; extract any pure\n  day/entry-resolution logic into a testable service, following the repo's\n  existing `src/lib/services/` pattern.\n\n## Why\n\nUsers have no home for day-based writing: notes are name-addressed folders, not\ndays. The journal gives one durable, always-present place to open today and\nwrite, reach yesterday for retroactive entries, and browse older days — the\nanchor of the connectivity direction.\n\n## Out of scope\n\n- **Backend/API work.** The journal folder, its flags, `is_system` immutability\n  and the hidden toggle already shipped in the API job\n  (`home_notebook-journal-folders/`); this job consumes that contract.\n- Calendar day-marks / check-in linking (direction step 2) and the planner.\n- A journal-specific note endpoint or a date field on notes — entries stay\n  ordinary notes addressed by the title convention.\n- Adopting or renaming pre-existing `.journal` folders; recursive/descendant\n  hiding; search over journal entries.\n- Exposing the hidden toggle for ordinary folders in the UI (the endpoint is\n  wired, but only the journal uses it in this job).\n\n## Notes\n\n- Journal identity is the folder's `is_system` flag, not its title, so a rename\n  (which the API forbids anyway) can't break the interface. Resolve the folder id\n  from `GET /notes/categories` and keep it out of the rendered tree.\n- The date convention is `getUrlFormat` (`YYYY-MM-DD`), the same as check-in;\n  entry titles equal the date. `NoteExportService` already exports the journal\n  folder — nothing to change there.\n- The dashboard's recent-notes widget reads `GET /notes/newest`, which the API\n  already filters; the notebook overview is computed client-side from\n  `GET /notes` and must be filtered here.\n- UI decisions the direction leaves open, to settle during implementation:\n  today opens write-ready, a past day opens the same editor read-first (no forced\n  focus); the datepicker marks days with entries and disables future days. These\n  are not blockers.\n- Route/naming is a developer choice; the check-in date route\n  (`/check-in/date/[date]`) is a close precedent if a date-addressed route fits.\n- Verify with `npm run check`, `npm run lint`, `npm test`; keep the four i18n\n  tables in sync (`npm run check` enforces it) and add unit tests for any\n  extracted day/entry-resolution logic.","lineStart":1,"lineEnd":80,"totalLines":80,"truncated":false}},"title":"docs/jobs/exist_notebook-journal/brief.md","time":{"start":1791639342791,"end":1791639342894}},"id":"prt_12606cd2100172Ak6G2TTmx9CW","sessionID":"ses_ed9f94635ffee3zYlqCKAoMoF3","messageID":"msg_12606bdee001D4rU2JYTHuiuvP"}}
